  "metadata": {
    "title": "Tim Cook Says Chip Wars Will Take a Big Bite of Apple",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "THE GIST\nOur analysts just identified a stock with the potential to be the next Nvidia. Tell us how you invest and we'll show you why it's our #1 pick. Tap here.\nApple CEO Tim Cook has spent his entire career managing supply chains. He helped build Apple into the most sophisticated procurement operation in consumer electronics. But that dexterity and strategy doesn't hold a candle against memory prices.\nThis is Cook throwing his hands up in the air to say that chip prices are rising so fast, that iPhones, Macs, and iPads will become more expensive, and there's nothing Apple can do about it.\nWHAT HAPPENED\nApple has been trying to shield customers from the increases but that is now becoming unsustainable. Cook described the environment as a \"hundred-year flood,\" which is notable language from someone who has worked in tech supply chains since stints at IBM and Compaq.\nThe issue is entirely with memory chips. Manufacturers like Samsung and SK Hynix are prioritizing high-bandwidth memory (HBM) for AI servers, over consumer DRAM and NAND flash that go into every iPhone, Mac, and iPad. DRAM prices have surged up to a 40% rise this quarter alone, and Apple may be forced to accept increases of 50% or more to secure sufficient DRAM supply for its 2026 lineup.\nThe math of how that transfers to the iPhone is dramatic: 12GB of DRAM for the iPhone 17 Pro cost Apple $39 last year. For the iPhone 18 Pro, the same amount of DRAM could cost $145.That means Apple would need to raise the iPhone Pro price by $270 just to maintain current profit margins.\nWHY IT MATTERS\nOne stock. Nvidia-level potential. 30M+ investors trust Moby to find it first. Get the pick. Tap here.\nThe memory crunch is very real, but it's landing on Apple when the company has been battered by two years of tariff exposure. When Trump's reciprocal tariffs hit in April of last year, its supply chain diversification strategy — iPhones from India, AirPods from Vietnam, Macs from Malaysia — turned out to offer little protection, because all those countries were also hit. One Morgan Stanley analyst estimated Apple would need to raise U.S. prices by 17% to 18% to offset tariff costs.\nApple's India pivot is also proving ineffective as Indian factories remain critically dependent on Chinese-made subcomponents, including sensors, circuit boards, and batteries. Manufacturing costs in alternative hubs are running 5–10% higher than China due to the logistical burden of importing those components.\nThis memory tax is just an additional burden on top of the existing tariff headache. AI hyperscalers are signing three-to-five year supply agreements with massive cash prepayments. That's something Apple can't stomach, given its history of disciplined spending. All that means the company competes for constrained memory supply without the leverage that cloud giants wield.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "THE GIST",
      "Our analysts just identified a stock with the potential to be the next Nvidia. Tell us how you invest and we'll show you why it's our #1 pick. Tap here.",
      "Apple CEO Tim Cook has spent his entire career managing supply chains. He helped build Apple into the most sophisticated procurement operation in consumer electronics. But that dexterity and strategy doesn't hold a candle against memory prices.",
      "This is Cook throwing his hands up in the air to say that chip prices are rising so fast, that iPhones, Macs, and iPads will become more expensive, and there's nothing Apple can do about it.",
      "WHAT HAPPENED",
      "Apple has been trying to shield customers from the increases but that is now becoming unsustainable. Cook described the environment as a \"hundred-year flood,\" which is notable language from someone who has worked in tech supply chains since stints at IBM and Compaq.",
      "The issue is entirely with memory chips. Manufacturers like Samsung and SK Hynix are prioritizing high-bandwidth memory (HBM) for AI servers, over consumer DRAM and NAND flash that go into every iPhone, Mac, and iPad. DRAM prices have surged up to a 40% rise this quarter alone, and Apple may be forced to accept increases of 50% or more to secure sufficient DRAM supply for its 2026 lineup.",
      "The math of how that transfers to the iPhone is dramatic: 12GB of DRAM for the iPhone 17 Pro cost Apple $39 last year. For the iPhone 18 Pro, the same amount of DRAM could cost $145.That means Apple would need to raise the iPhone Pro price by $270 just to maintain current profit margins.",
      "WHY IT MATTERS",
      "One stock. Nvidia-level potential. 30M+ investors trust Moby to find it first. Get the pick. Tap here.",
      "The memory crunch is very real, but it's landing on Apple when the company has been battered by two years of tariff exposure. When Trump's reciprocal tariffs hit in April of last year, its supply chain diversification strategy — iPhones from India, AirPods from Vietnam, Macs from Malaysia — turned out to offer little protection, because all those countries were also hit. One Morgan Stanley analyst estimated Apple would need to raise U.S. prices by 17% to 18% to offset tariff costs.",
      "Apple's India pivot is also proving ineffective as Indian factories remain critically dependent on Chinese-made subcomponents, including sensors, circuit boards, and batteries. Manufacturing costs in alternative hubs are running 5–10% higher than China due to the logistical burden of importing those components.",
      "This memory tax is just an additional burden on top of the existing tariff headache. AI hyperscalers are signing three-to-five year supply agreements with massive cash prepayments. That's something Apple can't stomach, given its history of disciplined spending. All that means the company competes for constrained memory supply without the leverage that cloud giants wield."
    ],
